From 54b46d72516aefe9749af406b66145c277e9d717 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Felipe Correa da Silva Sanches <juca@members.fsf.org>
Date: Tue, 21 Jul 2026 00:43:10 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 25/25] kn5000: Add TEMPO/PROGRAM data wheel encoder input

Wire the rotary data wheel below the LCD as an interactive control that edits
the on-screen tempo (and, on parameter screens, the focused value/program
field), matching the real instrument.

On real hardware the wheel is NOT a control-panel-serial input: in steady state
the firmware reads it with a main-loop poll, Encoder_ValueScanAndSync (ROM
0xFC5761), which walks an encoder scan table at MainCPU DRAM 0x8E94 -- 3-byte
entries [record_id, delta, mask], 0xFF-terminated. A record_id of 0x19 selects
descriptor 0xED9C1E[0x19] = {A9,21,00,FF}, which emits SwbtWr event 0xA9 /
subcode 0x21 (DRAM 0xC07D=0x21, 0xC07E=signed delta), posts UI event 0x1C0001F,
and drives the focused widget's dial handler -- on the home screen that is the
tempo box. So the panel HLE deposits the wheel's per-detent entry directly into
that scan table rather than sending a control-panel packet.

The delta is sign-inverted: the firmware's acceleration curve at ROM 0xEA98E2
maps a positive entry delta to a DECREASE, so a clockwise detent writes a
negative delta to raise the tempo. Idle discipline: the firmware resets the
table to 0xFF each main-loop iteration after consuming it, so an entry is
re-presented on each detent and nothing is written while the wheel is still --
matching the real producer, which stages one entry per scanned detent.

The wheel is an infinite relative encoder whose absolute position is meaningless
to the firmware, so it is declared as an adjuster rather than IPT_DIAL, and the
device reads the raw adjuster field instead of the interpolated analog port
value. Successive positions are diffed wrap-aware and slewed one detent per
scan, turning a fast drag into a stream of single-detent entries. This mirrors
the relative-encoder handling in kn_cpanel.cpp used by the KN7000 and KN6000,
although those machines use a completely different consumer (a signed step byte
on wire 0x17 which their firmware accumulates).

The layout knob is interactive: a circular drag, or the mouse wheel while
hovering, spins the finger grip and steps the encoder 24 notches per revolution,
using the same shared slider/knob library the KN7000 and KN6000 layouts use.

Depositing the scan-table entry was verified live to drive the on-screen tempo
one clean BPM step per detent, up for clockwise and down for counter-clockwise,
with the display refreshing and the value holding steady when the wheel stops.
This is a high-level model of the tone-generator encoder register that feeds
DRAM 0x8E94; a fully device-accurate model would emulate that register so the
firmware's poll reads the wheel naturally.
